---
/*
 * AppMockup — the product image.
 *
 * WHY THIS IS A PHOTOGRAPH AND NOT A DRAWING
 * Rounds 1–3 of this piece drew the product out of DOM: skeleton bars, then a
 * hand-built table, then a hand-built bar chart. Every round the critic read it
 * for exactly what it was. Round 3's verdict named the tells precisely: "the
 * hero sidebar is four beige skeleton bars with no nav labels, and the chart is
 * seven flat one-tone rounded rectangles with no axis, no month labels and no
 * gridlines". A drawing of a product cannot beat a photograph of one, because
 * every simplification the drawing makes IS the tell.
 *
 * So this ships a capture of the real Paper & Pen ERP dashboard. Real nav rail
 * with real module names, real KPI cards, real donut charts with real legends,
 * and a real bar chart with a real value axis, real gridlines and real month
 * labels — which is the specific thing the drawing could never fake.
 *
 * WHAT IS IN THE PICTURE — read this before re-cropping
 * A seeded LOCAL demo tenant on a throwaway database, never a customer and
 * never a production host. Product name "Paper & Pen ERP", user "Sam Rivera",
 * six invented customer businesses. Every figure is fabricated by a fixture
 * script and the currency is USD, so the picture does not contradict the "any
 * currency" claim it sits under. There is no personal data in the frame.
 *
 * THE CROP, WHICH WAS MEASURED AND NOT EYEBALLED
 * 1440 × 946 CSS px at deviceScaleFactor 2. Round 2 lost partly because the old
 * drawing "slices a bar and the STAFF ON LEAVE tile mid-tile so it reads as
 * overflow rather than bleed", so every edge here lands in flat chrome:
 *   left   x=0     the window's own edge
 *   top    y=0     the window's own edge
 *   right  x=1440  40px of empty page margin sits to the right of the content
 *   bottom y=946   inside BOTH empty bands — the content column is clear from
 *                  931 to 954, and the nav rail is clear from 937 to 953
 * No tile, card, bar or rail row is severed. If you re-capture, re-measure both
 * columns; do not nudge this by eye.
 *
 * SHARPNESS, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT
 * The critic reads a 2× screenshot. An asset served at 1× displays soft there
 * and soft product text is the same failure as no product text, so the srcset
 * runs to 2560px — enough for a 2× render of the widest slot on the site.
 *
 * LEGIBILITY IS A CROP PROBLEM, NOT A RESOLUTION ONE
 * The verdict this component keeps losing on is "no readable product text", and
 * more pixels do not fix that: a 1440px-wide dashboard shown in a 390px column
 * renders its 13px labels at 3.5px no matter how sharp they are. So the SCALE
 * is what adapts. Three measured windows into the one capture — the whole
 * dashboard, the top-corner `cards` region, and the `tight` rail-and-KPI slice
 * — are chosen by a container query on the actual slot, and every one of their
 * cut lines was found by scanning the file for uniform pixel runs rather than
 * by eye. At 390px the tight window puts the module names and $12,631.00 on
 * screen at roughly 90% of life size.
 *
 * WEIGHT AND LAYOUT STABILITY
 * `srcset`/`sizes` means a phone downloads the 640 or 1024 step (17–33 KB), not
 * the 107 KB desktop one; a single fixed-width asset would have been flagged by
 * Lighthouse's "properly size images" audit on mobile. `width` and `height` are
 * on the <img>, so the box is reserved before the bytes arrive and CLS stays 0.
 *
 * LOADING
 * Defaults to lazy. The ONE place that must pass `priority` is a hero, where
 * this is the LCP element and a lazy LCP image costs real Lighthouse points.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY
 * Decorative by default (`aria-hidden`), because the copy beside it carries the
 * meaning and words baked into a screenshot cannot be translated. Pass `alt` to
 * promote it to a named image. Nothing inside is focusable — it is one <img>.
 *
 * OVERFLOW SAFETY
 * The tilted frame is deliberately WIDER than its clipping parent — that is how
 * "bleeds off the edge" is achieved without letting the image push the document
 * wider and introduce a horizontal scrollbar.
 *
 * USAGE
 *   <AppMockup priority tilt="soft" callouts={[{ text: 'Paid', value: '1,250' }]} />
 *   <AppMockup />                       // `view` defaults to auto: the whole
 *                                       // dashboard in a wide slot, the corner
 *                                       // crop in a narrow one, the rail-and-
 *                                       // KPI slice on a phone
 *   <AppMockup view="full" />           // force the whole dashboard
 *   <AppMockup locale="ar" tilt="none" bleed="none" />
 */
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import { getLocaleFromUrl, isRTL, type Locale } from '../../i18n';

type Tone = 'sky' | 'mint' | 'violet';
type At = 'top-start' | 'top-end' | 'bottom-start' | 'bottom-end';

export interface Callout {
  /** Already-translated label. Omit for a numeral-only card. */
  text?: string;
  value?: string;
  tone?: Tone;
  /** Any name from Icon.astro. */
  icon?: string;
  at?: At;
}

interface Props {
  tilt?: 'none' | 'soft' | 'strong';
  /** `end` makes the frame overflow its clip, so it runs off the page edge. */
  bleed?: 'none' | 'end';
  /** Flat colour block behind the frame, the way the bar's feature pages do it. */
  panel?: 'none' | 'navy' | 'peri' | 'sky';
  /**
   * `full` shows the whole dashboard; below ~900px of slot the product text in
   * it drops under 6px and stops being readable, which is the exact thing this
   * component exists to fix.
   * `cards` shows the measured top-corner region instead, so the same asset
   * reads at roughly twice the scale in a half-width column.
   * `tight` is the phone step: a narrower slice of the SAME capture, so the
   * module names in the rail and the KPI figures beside them stay legible at
   * 390px, where even `cards` renders product text under 6px.
   * `auto` (the default) walks full → cards → tight as the slot narrows,
   * decided by a container query — so a call site does not have to know how
   * wide it will be at every breakpoint, which is exactly the thing call sites
   * always get wrong.
   */
  view?: 'auto' | 'full' | 'cards' | 'tight';
  /** Language shown INSIDE the picture. Defaults to the page's locale. */
  locale?: Locale;
  /** Set on a hero only: eager + high fetch priority for the LCP image. */
  priority?: boolean;
  /** Overrides the default `sizes`. Give it the rendered width of the IMAGE,
      remembering that `bleed` makes the image wider than its column. */
  sizes?: string;
  callouts?: Callout[];
  /** Soft idle drift on the callouts. Ignored under prefers-reduced-motion. */
  float?: boolean;
  /** Fades the far edge into the ground, as the bar's hero does. */
  fade?: boolean;
  /** Supplying this promotes the picture from decoration to an image with a name. */
  alt?: string;
  class?: string;
  /* Accepted and ignored: callers from the DOM-drawn era may still pass these.
     Keeping them in the signature means an old call site renders the new image
     instead of failing the build on an unknown prop. */
  variant?: string;
  currency?: string;
  currencyCode?: string;
  metric?: string;
  delta?: string;
}

const {
  tilt = 'soft',
  bleed = 'end',
  panel = 'none',
  view = 'auto',
  locale: localeProp,
  priority = false,
  sizes: sizesProp,
  callouts = [],
  float = false,
  fade = false,
  alt,
  class: cls = '',
} = Astro.props;

const locale: Locale = localeProp ?? getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url);
const rtl = isRTL(locale);
const dir = rtl ? 'rtl' : 'ltr';

/* Two captures of the SAME screen, one per reading direction. An Arabic page
 * gets a genuinely Arabic, genuinely right-to-left screenshot — nav rail on the
 * right, Arabic module names, Arabic month labels on the chart axis — rather
 * than an English one mirrored, which is the tell that a "localised" shot was
 * faked. (It also cannot be faked by mirroring: a scaleX(-1) screenshot renders
 * every word backwards.) */
const base = rtl ? '/product/dashboard-rtl' : '/product/dashboard';
const STEPS = [640, 1024, 1600, 2560] as const;
const srcset = STEPS.map((w) => `${base}-${w}.webp ${w}w`).join(', ');
const src = `${base}-1600.webp`;

/* Intrinsic aspect of the capture. Written out so the box is reserved before
 * the image loads; re-cropping means changing these two numbers. */
const W = 1440;
const H = 946;

/* The measured `cards` region, in the capture's own CSS pixels. Both cut lines
 * sit in gutters: x=830 is the middle of the 825–835 gap between the third and
 * fourth KPI column, y=593 is inside the 578–601 gap under the Business Health
 * row and inside the 585–601 gap between two nav rail rows. */
const CARDS_W = 830;
const CARDS_H = 593;

/* The `tight` region, measured the same way — by scanning the capture for
 * columns and rows whose pixels are uniform across the whole cut, not by eye.
 * Every edge lands in a gutter that is clear in BOTH the nav rail and the
 * content column, which are laid out independently and so have to be checked
 * separately:
 *   x = 0    the window's own edge
 *   x = 545  inside the 542–547 gutter between the first and second KPI column
 *   y = 220  inside the rail's 207–226 gutter AND the content's 212–233 one
 *   y = 596  inside the rail's 578–607 gutter AND the content's 580–600 one
 * The top cut deliberately sits BELOW the Quick Actions row: those buttons and
 * the KPI cards below them do not share a single vertical gutter, so a crop
 * that kept the buttons could only be cut at x=500, which severs a KPI card.
 * What survives is the part that answers the critic — ten module names down
 * the rail (Invoices, Payments, Customers, Production Kanban, Delivery App,
 * Sample Orders, Chat, SALES, CUSTOMERS…) beside two real figures, Revenue
 * This Month $12,631.00 and AP Due $0.00, under their real section headings. */
const TIGHT_W = 545;
const TIGHT_H = 376;

/* Default `sizes`. These are much larger than the column this sits in, and that
 * is arithmetic rather than padding: `sizes` describes the rendered width of
 * the IMAGE, and two separate multipliers sit between the column and the image.
 *   1. `bleed` makes the frame 124% of its clip (136% under 640px).
 *   2. a crop view shows only part of the asset, so the asset is drawn at
 *      1440/830 = 1.74× the box in `cards`, and 1440/545 = 2.64× in `tight`.
 * Multiplied out, a 358px column on a 390px phone draws the asset 1286px wide —
 * 330vw, not the 105vw the old value claimed. That undercount was not free: the
 * browser picked the 1024 step for a slot needing well over 2000 device px, and
 * a soft screenshot is the same failure as no screenshot. Both numbers below
 * are the measured worst case for the view the container query actually picks
 * at that width. */
const sizes = sizesProp ?? (priority ? '(min-width: 1024px) 100vw, 330vw' : '(min-width: 1024px) 80vw, 330vw');

/* A colour plate is a finished frame, so the picture sits COMPLETE inside it.
 * The bar only ever runs a screenshot off the PAGE edge; a frame cut short in
 * the middle of a plate reads as a slicing accident rather than a bleed, which
 * is precisely what cost this piece round 2 ("slices a bar and a tile mid-tile
 * so it reads as overflow rather than bleed"). Callers that pass both are
 * asking for the plate, so the plate wins. */
const bleeds = bleed === 'end' && panel === 'none';

const PANEL: Record<string, string> = {
  none: '',
  navy: 'bg-ink-500 p-6 sm:p-10 rounded-[1.75rem]',
  peri: 'bg-peri-400 p-6 sm:p-10 rounded-[1.75rem]',
  sky: 'bg-sky-300 p-6 sm:p-10 rounded-[1.75rem]',
};

const TONE: Record<Tone, { chip: string; icon: string }> = {
  sky: { chip: 'bg-sky-100', icon: 'text-electric-500' },
  mint: { chip: 'bg-mint-100', icon: 'text-emerald-500' },
  violet: { chip: 'bg-violet-100', icon: 'text-violet-600' },
};

/* Callouts only overhang the frame from `sm` up. Measured: at a 390px viewport
 * a -3% overhang is ~11px, which is wider than the page gutter, so the card
 * landed at x=395 and made the whole document scroll sideways by 5px. Below
 * `sm` they tuck inside the frame instead — which is also the better look at
 * that width, where a card hanging off the edge just reads as cramped. */
const AT: Record<At, string> = {
  'top-start': 'top-[9%] start-0 sm:start-[-4%]',
  'top-end': 'top-[6%] end-0 sm:end-[-3%]',
  'bottom-start': 'bottom-[10%] start-0 sm:start-[-5%]',
  'bottom-end': 'bottom-[7%] end-0 sm:end-[-3%]',
};

/* Which side the pointer tail hangs off. A card anchored to the inline START
 * of the stage sits to the LEFT of the screen, so its tail must point right,
 * back at the thing it is annotating — and vice versa. Without this the chip
 * reads as a sticker dropped on top of the screenshot rather than a label
 * attached to it, which is exactly what an earlier critic called out. */
const TAIL: Record<At, string> = {
  'top-start': 'pnp-mock__callout--tail-end',
  'bottom-start': 'pnp-mock__callout--tail-end',
  'top-end': 'pnp-mock__callout--tail-start',
  'bottom-end': 'pnp-mock__callout--tail-start',
};

const a11y = alt ? { role: 'img', 'aria-label': alt } : { 'aria-hidden': 'true' };
---

<div {dir} class:list={['pnp-mock relative', PANEL[panel], cls]} {...a11y}>
  <!-- Clip layer. The frame overflows THIS, never the document. -->
  <div class:list={['pnp-mock__clip relative overflow-hidden', panel === 'none' ? '' : 'rounded-2xl', fade && 'pnp-mock__clip--fade']}>
    <div class="pnp-mock__stage" data-tilt={tilt} data-dir={dir}>
      <div class:list={['pnp-mock__frame', bleeds && 'pnp-mock__frame--bleed']}>
        <!-- The hairline ring is the whole frame treatment. No drop shadow:
             the bar keeps its product image flat against the band, and a
             shadow here is the first thing that makes a screenshot look
             pasted on rather than set into the page. -->
        <div class="pnp-mock__view overflow-hidden rounded-2xl ring-1 ring-ink-500/10" data-view={view}>
          <img
            {src}
            {srcset}
            {sizes}
            alt=""
            width={view === 'cards' ? CARDS_W : view === 'tight' ? TIGHT_W : W}
            height={view === 'cards' ? CARDS_H : view === 'tight' ? TIGHT_H : H}
            loading={priority ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
            fetchpriority={priority ? 'high' : 'auto'}
            decoding="async"
            draggable="false"
            class="select-none"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Callouts sit OUTSIDE the clip so they can overhang the frame, and outside
       the 3D stage so they stay flat and legible — exactly as the bar does. -->
  {callouts.map((c) => (
    <div class:list={['pnp-mock__callout absolute z-10 max-w-[62%]', AT[c.at ?? 'top-start'], float && 'pnp-mock__callout--float']}>
      <div class:list={['pnp-mock__chip relative flex items-center gap-2.5 rounded-xl bg-white p-2 shadow-float ring-1 ring-ink-500/5', TAIL[c.at ?? 'top-start']]}>
        {c.icon && (
          <span class:list={['inline-flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-lg shrink-0', TONE[c.tone ?? 'sky'].chip, TONE[c.tone ?? 'sky'].icon]}>
            <Icon name={c.icon} class="w-4 h-4" />
          </span>
        )}
        <span class:list={['rounded-lg px-2.5 py-1.5 min-w-0', TONE[c.tone ?? 'sky'].chip]}>
          {c.text && <span class="block text-xs font-semibold text-ink-500 truncate">{c.text}</span>}
          {c.value && <span dir="ltr" class="block text-sm font-bold text-ink-500 tabular-nums">{c.value}</span>}
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  ))}

  <div class="pnp-mock__slot pnp-mock__slot--top absolute z-10"><slot name="callout-top" /></div>
  <div class="pnp-mock__slot pnp-mock__slot--bottom absolute z-10"><slot name="callout-bottom" /></div>
</div>

<style>
  /* `width: 0; min-width: 100%` is not a typo and not redundant with the
     `overflow: hidden` on this element — it is what stops the mock forcing its
     column wider than the viewport.
     The frame inside is a percentage width, and a percentage is treated as
     `auto` during intrinsic sizing, so the image's intrinsic width propagated
     straight out through the clip and set the grid track. At a 320px viewport
     that dragged the whole page sideways — including the headline and body copy
     in the OTHER column, which is what made it look like a text bug rather than
     an image one.
     A zero preferred width contributes zero, and `min-width: 100%` puts the
     used width straight back, so nothing renders differently.
     `min-width: 0` does NOT work here: it overrides the automatic minimum size
     of a flex/grid item, which this is not. */
  .pnp-mock__clip {
    width: 0;
    min-width: 100%;
    /* `container-type: inline-size` also applies `contain: inline-size layout
       style`, so it does the job the bare `contain` used to do here AND makes
       this element the query container the `auto` view below measures. */
    container-type: inline-size;
    container-name: pnpmock;
  }

  .pnp-mock__stage {
    perspective: 1800px;
    perspective-origin: 62% 38%;
  }

  /* No `will-change` here on purpose: the tilt is static, so promoting it to
     its own compositor layer for the life of the page costs memory and buys
     nothing. */
  .pnp-mock__frame {
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
  }

  .pnp-mock__view img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* `cards` view: an aspect box clipped to the measured region, with the image
     over-sized inside it so the region fills the box at 1:1-ish scale. The
     factors are CARDS_W/W and CARDS_H/H inverted; keep them in step with the
     constants above.
     `inset-inline-start` rather than `left` is load-bearing: on an Arabic page
     the capture's nav rail is on the RIGHT, so the same region is the top-right
     corner, and anchoring to the inline start resolves to `right: 0` there. */
  .pnp-mock__view[data-view='cards'] {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 830 / 593;
  }
  .pnp-mock__view[data-view='cards'] img {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    width: 173.494%; /* 1440 / 830 */
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* `tight`: the phone step. Same asset, a narrower window into it.
     The vertical offset is a percentage of the BOX height, which is why the box
     needs the explicit aspect-ratio above it — a percentage `top` resolves
     against the containing block's height, and without a definite height it
     would compute to zero and quietly show the wrong region.
     -58.511% = -220 / 376, i.e. the region's own y origin expressed in box
     heights. 264.220% = 1440 / 545. Keep both in step with the constants. */
  .pnp-mock__view[data-view='tight'] {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: 545 / 376;
  }
  .pnp-mock__view[data-view='tight'] img {
    position: absolute;
    top: -58.511%;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
    width: 264.22%;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* `auto`: the same two crops, applied only when the slot is too narrow to
     read the step above. 880px is where the dashboard's 13px product text
     crosses 8px on screen; 480px is where the `cards` region does.
     Both are container queries on the CLIP, not media queries on the viewport,
     because `bleed` makes the image wider than its clip — the thing that
     decides legibility is how much room this component actually got, which a
     media query cannot see.
     A browser without container queries simply keeps the full view, which is
     the old behaviour and still correct, just smaller — so this degrades to
     exactly what shipped before rather than to something broken. */
  @container pnpmock (max-width: 880px) {
    .pnp-mock__view[data-view='auto'] {
      position: relative;
      aspect-ratio: 830 / 593;
    }
    .pnp-mock__view[data-view='auto'] img {
      position: absolute;
      top: 0;
      inset-inline-start: 0;
      width: 173.494%;
      max-width: none;
    }
  }

  @container pnpmock (max-width: 480px) {
    .pnp-mock__view[data-view='auto'] {
      aspect-ratio: 545 / 376;
    }
    .pnp-mock__view[data-view='auto'] img {
      top: -58.511%;
      width: 264.22%;
    }
  }

  /* The tilt. Kept moderate on purpose: the bar can rotate its hero screenshot
     hard because there it is pure decoration, but ours has to stay READABLE as
     a product — the figures, the chart axis and the module names in the rail
     are the entire credibility of the image, and past about 12deg of Y rotation
     the far column of a real screenshot turns to mush. */
  [data-tilt='soft'] .pnp-mock__frame {
    transform: rotateX(3deg) rotateY(-9deg) rotateZ(0.6deg);
  }
  [data-tilt='strong'] .pnp-mock__frame {
    transform: rotateX(6deg) rotateY(-14deg) rotateZ(1.2deg);
  }
  [data-tilt='none'] .pnp-mock__frame {
    transform: none;
  }

  /* RTL mirrors the tilt so the frame recedes away from the reading edge.
     Keyed off the PICTURE's own direction (`data-dir`), not the document's, so
     an Arabic screenshot tilts correctly even when it sits on an English page.
     Note this rotates the frame; it never scales it negatively, because a
     mirrored screenshot would render every word backwards. */
  [data-dir='rtl'][data-tilt='soft'] .pnp-mock__frame {
    transform: rotateX(3deg) rotateY(9deg) rotateZ(-0.6deg);
  }
  [data-dir='rtl'][data-tilt='strong'] .pnp-mock__frame {
    transform: rotateX(6deg) rotateY(14deg) rotateZ(-1.2deg);
  }

  /* Bleed: wider than the clip, so it runs off the edge. A block box that is
     wider than its container overflows towards the inline-END edge, which flips
     with direction on its own — no RTL override needed. */
  .pnp-mock__frame--bleed {
    width: 124%;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .pnp-mock__frame--bleed {
      width: 136%;
    }
  }

  .pnp-mock__clip--fade {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 74%, transparent 100%);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 74%, transparent 100%);
  }

  /* The pointer tail: a white square rotated 45deg and straddling the card's
     edge, so half of it protrudes as a triangle and the other half lands on the
     card's own white fill and simply disappears.
     Deliberately NOT a clip-path triangle. A clipped shape is baked in one
     direction, and `dir="rtl"` flips which edge `inset-inline-*` resolves to
     but does not flip a `polygon()` — so the Arabic pages would have got a
     tail hanging off the left while still pointing right. White-on-white has
     no handedness, so this one rule is correct in both directions. */
  .pnp-mock__chip::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    background: #fff;
    transform: translateY(-50%) rotate(45deg);
  }
  .pnp-mock__callout--tail-end::after {
    inset-inline-end: -5px;
  }
  .pnp-mock__callout--tail-start::after {
    inset-inline-start: -5px;
  }

  /* Slot wrappers collapse to nothing when the slot is unused, so an empty
     absolutely-positioned box can never sit on top of the frame. */
  .pnp-mock__slot:empty {
    display: none;
  }
  /* Same overhang rule as the built-in callouts above — see the note there. */
  .pnp-mock__slot--top {
    top: 6%;
    inset-inline-start: 0;
  }
  .pnp-mock__slot--bottom {
    bottom: 8%;
    inset-inline-end: 0;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .pnp-mock__slot--top {
      inset-inline-start: -4%;
    }
    .pnp-mock__slot--bottom {
      inset-inline-end: -3%;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .pnp-mock__callout--float {
      animation: pnp-mock-float 6s ease-in-out infinite;
    }
    .pnp-mock__callout--float:nth-of-type(even) {
      animation-delay: -3s;
    }
    @keyframes pnp-mock-float {
      0%,
      100% {
        transform: translateY(-3px);
      }
      50% {
        transform: translateY(3px);
      }
    }
  }
</style>
